  <div class="copy">
    <p class="tag reveal">03 · Trunk</p>
    <h2 class="reveal" data-d="1" id="who-we-are-title">Pamoja means together.</h2>
    <p class="reveal" data-d="2">
      Pamoja Cultural Collective is a grassroots collective led by newcomer
      Canadians of African Muslim origin, building community on ethical common
      ground in Hamilton. The name is Kiswahili, and it is the whole argument
      in one word: together — not merged, not assimilated, together.
    </p>
    <h3 class="reveal sub-head" data-d="3">Our mission, including the honest parts.</h3>
    <p class="reveal" data-d="3">
      Pamoja exists so that the knowledge, wisdom, and traditions newcomers
      carry become part of how Hamilton understands and builds itself. We
      convene, we host, we teach, we braid people and efforts together — and
      the city is changed by what its newcomers bring to it.
    </p>
    <p class="reveal" data-d="4">
      Two honest things belong here. First: belonging is not comfort, and it
      is not sameness. Belonging means embracing discomfort as a path to
      understanding — ours included. Second: we do not claim to be everything
      to everyone. We are one collective with a specific practice and specific
      roots, working in a city full of other good work.
    </p>
    <a class="cta reveal" data-d="4" href="#our-work">Follow the branches →</a>
  </div>

  <div class="why-we-exist reveal" style="grid-column: 1 / -1">
    <h3 class="sub-head">Why we exist.</h3>
    <p>
      Canada welcomes newcomers for their labour — and too often exploits that
      labour in menial jobs while the knowledge those same people carry goes
      unused. A physician drives a cab. A teacher stacks shelves. A community
      that governed itself for generations is asked to wait quietly for
      services. The welcome and the waste are the same story, and it is told
      every day in this city.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pamoja is our answer to it. We do not wait to be served, and we refuse
      to be only labour. We host. We teach. We build. What we carry — the
      coffee ritual, the tea gathering, the ethics of Ta'aruf and Takaful —
      is something Hamilton needs, and we bring it as its holders, not its
      applicants.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- WORDING NOTICE: the block below carries the full reasoning of brief
       §1.1. Final wording to be supplied/approved by Abdo. -->
  <section class="what-we-are reveal" style="grid-column: 1 / -1" aria-labelledby="what-we-are-heading">
    <h3 id="what-we-are-heading">What we are, and what we're not.</h3>
    <p>
      Hamilton has many settlement organizations. They do essential work —
      work ours would not be possible without — and we collaborate with them
      respectfully. But they are not what we are.
    </p>
    <p>
      The difference is the direction of the relationship. A settlement
      organization works <em>for</em> newcomers — delivering services to
      people understood as needing them. Pamoja works <em>with</em>
      newcomers to build this city — treating the knowledge, wisdom, and
      traditions newcomers carry as something Hamilton needs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Same city, same people, different direction. Our work surfaces what
      newcomers bring and builds the city together with them; it does not
      deliver services to a client group. The critique above and this
      distinction are one thought: people welcomed for their labour and
      exploited in menial jobs are the same people whose wisdom this city
      has not yet learned to receive.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="braids" style="grid-column: 1 / -1">
    <h3 class="sub-head reveal">The four braids.</h3>
    <p class="reveal" data-d="1">
      Everything Pamoja does braids four strands together. No event carries
      only one.
    </p>
    <ol class="braid-list">
      <li class="reveal" data-d="1">
        <h4>Decolonial education</h4>
        <p>
          Knowledge shared on its own terms — community knowledge holders
          teaching what they carry, unmediated and uncorrected by the frames
          that usually receive it.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="reveal" data-d="2">
        <h4>Intergenerational dialogue</h4>
        <p>
          Elders, parents, and young people in the same conversation, so that
          what is carried across an ocean is not lost between generations.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="reveal" data-d="3">
        <h4>Mutual economic empowerment</h4>
        <p>
          Takaful in practice — honourariums, shared resources, and collective
          stewardship that treat prosperity as something held in common.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="reveal" data-d="4">
        <h4>Community connection</h4>
        <p>
          The plain fact of people meeting as equals — hosting, eating,
          talking, making — until a city of strangers becomes a network of
          collaborators.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="origin" style="grid-column: 1 / -1">
    <h3 class="sub-head reveal">Where we grew from.</h3>
    <p class="reveal" data-d="1">
      Pamoja is rooted in Sudanese traditions of hospitality — the jebena
      coffee ceremony, the tea poured for whoever arrives, the seat that is
      always made. Those traditions found fertile ground in Hamilton at the
      Community Permaculture Lab, whose front yard became our first home.
    </p>
    <p class="reveal" data-d="2">
      What began there as a biweekly tea gathering grew, gathering by
      gathering, into a collective. The tea gathering itself has since been
      handed on — it is now fully owned and run by the Community Permaculture
      Lab, which is what growth looks like when the roots take.
    </p>
    <p class="next-step reveal" data-d="3">
      See what the tree has grown: <a href="#our-work">our work</a>, and the
      people in <a href="#who-we-grow-with">the grove around it</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
